Since you're on **.NET 10**, the built-in `TimeProvider` class is the right abstraction — no custom wrapper needed.

## How to adopt `TimeProvider`

### 1. Inject `TimeProvider` into your class

```csharp
public class ReportGenerator(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public string GenerateReport(string reportName)
    {
        var timestamp = timeProvider.GetUtcNow().ToString("yyyyMMdd_HHmmss");
        // ...
        var content = $"Report: {reportName}\nGenerated: {timeProvider.GetUtcNow():O}\n...";
        // ...
    }
}
```

### 2. Register in DI (`Program.cs`)

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton(TimeProvider.System);
```

### 3. Mock in tests with `FakeTimeProvider`

Install the testing package:
```
dotnet add <TestProject> package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```

Then in your test:
```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing;

var fakeTime = new FakeTimeProvider(
    new DateTimeOffset(2026, 6, 24, 12, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));

var generator = new ReportGenerator(fakeTime);
var path = generator.GenerateReport("Sales");

Assert.Contains("20260624_120000", path); // deterministic!
fakeTime.Advance(TimeSpan.FromHours(1));  // advance as needed
```

### Key points
- `TimeProvider.System` is the production implementation (replaces `DateTime.UtcNow`)
- `FakeTimeProvider` lets you freeze/advance time in tests
- No custom interface needed — it's built into the BCL since .NET 8

